%N A402126 Least s such that some s X s grid of lattice points with spacing prime(n) shows a pattern of visible and invisible points that no s X s grid with spacing 1 shows.
%C A402126 A lattice point (x, y) with positive integer coordinates is visible from the origin if gcd(x, y) = 1, that is, if no other lattice point lies strictly between it and the origin. For positive integers s, d, A, B, the grid of side s, spacing d and corner (A, B) consists of the s^2 points (A + d*i, B + d*j) with integers 0 <= i, j < s (so s is the number of points in each row and column). Its pattern is the s X s array that has a 1 at position (i, j) if the point (A + d*i, B + d*j) is visible, and a 0 otherwise. For a prime p, R(p) is the least s such that some grid of side s and spacing p has a pattern that no grid of side s and spacing 1 has, whatever its corner; such an s exists for every prime p. Then a(n) = R(prime(n)).
%C A402126 For every odd prime p <= 139, R(p) = 2*p - 1. The equality fails at p = 149 (R(149) = 327, not 297), holds again at p = 151 (R(151) = 301), and fails for every prime p > 151. So for odd primes p, R(p) = 2*p - 1 if and only if p <= 139 or p = 151. In particular the sequence is not nondecreasing: a(35) = 327 > a(36) = 301.
%C A402126 Why spacing 3 can show a pattern that spacing 1 cannot: group the positions (i, j) of a pattern into nine classes according to the pair of remainders (i mod 3, j mod 3). In a grid with spacing 1 the points with both coordinates divisible by 3 are invisible, and they fill one whole class. So a pattern with a 1 in each of the nine classes never occurs with spacing 1; with spacing 3 it can occur (see the Example section). In general, by a theorem of Herzog and Stewart, a finite pattern of 0's and 1's occurs with spacing 1 if and only if for every prime q at least one of the q^2 classes of positions modulo q contains no 1 of the pattern.
%C A402126 The same criterion holds for spacing p, except that the prime q = p imposes no condition. Consequently R(p) is the least s for which the following is possible: for each prime q < p choose one class of positions modulo q and delete its positions from the s X s array of positions (i, j), 0 <= i, j < s, in such a way that each of the p^2 classes modulo p still contains an undeleted position. (A pattern with a 1 at one undeleted position of each class modulo p, and 0 elsewhere, then occurs with spacing p but not with spacing 1; it has only p^2 ones, so the primes q > p cause no obstruction.) For odd p, the class deleted for q = 2 alone forces s >= 2*p - 1.
%C A402126 For every prime p >= 211, R(p) >= 3*p - 209. Moreover R(p)/p tends to infinity: for all sufficiently large primes p, R(p) lies between two constant multiples of f(p) = p*sqrt(log(p)/log(log(p))) (see the Formula section). These bounds do not establish whether R(p)/f(p) has a limit.
%C A402126 The numbers 2*p - 1, p prime, are in A076274: a(n) = A076274(n+1) exactly for 2 <= n <= 34 and n = 36. The patterns with spacing 1 are the contiguous square subarrays of the array A054431. A one-dimensional relative is A058989 (the longest run of consecutive integers each divisible by at least one of the first n primes): its terms with indices 3 to 8 agree with a(2), ..., a(7) = 5, 9, 13, 21, 25, 33, and then A058989(9) = 39 whereas a(8) = 37. See the notes file in the Links section for proofs, sources and further data.
