%F A402126 a(1) = 2.
%F A402126 a(n) >= 2*prime(n) - 1 for n >= 2, with equality if and only if 2 <= n <= 34 or n = 36.
%F A402126 a(n) >= 3*prime(n) - 209 for n >= 47.
%F A402126 Pi/sqrt(6) <= liminf_{n->oo} a(n)/f(prime(n)) <= limsup_{n->oo} a(n)/f(prime(n)) <= Pi/sqrt(3), where f(p) = p*sqrt(log(p)/log(log(p))) for primes p >= 3.
%e A402126 n = 1 (p = 2): the grid of side 2, spacing 2 and corner (1, 2) consists of the points (1, 2), (1, 4), (3, 2), (3, 4). All four are visible, so its pattern consists of four 1's. A grid of side 2 and spacing 1 always contains a point with both coordinates even, so its pattern contains a 0. Both patterns of side 1 occur with spacing 1: (1) at the corner (1, 1) and (0) at the corner (2, 2). So a(1) = 2.
%e A402126 n = 2 (p = 3): the grid of side 5, spacing 3 and corner (1, 2) consists of the points (x, y) with x = 1, 4, 7, 10, 13 (rows i = 0..4) and y = 2, 5, 8, 11, 14 (columns j = 0..4). Its pattern is
%e A402126   1 1 1 1 1
%e A402126   0 1 0 1 0
%e A402126   1 1 1 1 0
%e A402126   0 0 0 1 0
%e A402126   1 1 1 1 1
%e A402126 (for example, gcd(4, 5) = 1 gives a 1, and gcd(4, 8) = 4 gives a 0). The nine positions with i = 0, 2, 4 and j = 0, 1, 2 all hold a 1, and they represent all nine classes modulo 3. So this pattern does not occur with spacing 1, and R(3) <= 5. Since R(3) >= 2*3 - 1, a(2) = 5.
%e A402126 n = 3 (p = 5): take s = 9. Delete the positions (i, j) with i and j both odd (a class modulo 2) and those with i and j both divisible by 3 (a class modulo 3). Modulo 5, the index 4 is alone in its class of indices, and every other index has one partner (0 and 5, 1 and 6, 2 and 7, 3 and 8). A class of positions modulo 5 that involves the index 4 loses nothing, because 4 is neither odd nor divisible by 3. Every other class has four positions, of which the first deletion removes exactly one and the second at most one. So every class modulo 5 keeps a position, and R(5) <= 9. Since R(5) >= 2*5 - 1, a(3) = 9.
%o A402126 (Python)
%o A402126 # A402126: exhaustive search with the reformulation in the Comments
%o A402126 # (one deleted class for each smaller prime), for the first eight primes.
%o A402126 from itertools import count
%o A402126 P = [2, 3, 5, 7, 11, 13, 17, 19]
%o A402126 def possible(p, s):
%o A402126     fibers = [sum(1 << (i*s+j) for i in range(a,s,p) for j in range(b,s,p)) for a in range(p) for b in range(p)]
%o A402126     choices = [[sum(1 << (i*s+j) for i in range(a,s,q) for j in range(b,s,q)) for a in range(q) for b in range(q)] for q in P if q < p]
%o A402126     def search(k, live):
%o A402126         if not all(live & f for f in fibers): return False
%o A402126         if k == len(choices): return True
%o A402126         return any(search(k+1, live & ~cut) for cut in choices[k])
%o A402126     return search(0, (1 << (s*s))-1)
%o A402126 for p in P:
%o A402126     print(next(s for s in count(p) if possible(p,s)))
